00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:40.290
But if you have never done
anything with neural networks,

00:01:40.290 --> 00:01:45.510
that's fine and I will explain
everything from the start.

00:01:45.510 --> 00:01:48.360
So this is the simplest
possible neural network

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:52.150
we can imagine to recognize
our hand-written digits.

00:01:52.150 --> 00:01:56.130
So the digits, they come
as 28 by 28 pixel images

00:01:56.130 --> 00:01:58.350
and the first thing we
do is that we flatten

00:01:58.350 --> 00:02:01.380
all those pixels into
one big vector of pixels

00:02:01.380 --> 00:02:03.900
and these will be our inputs.

00:02:03.900 --> 00:02:08.620
Now, we will use
exactly 10 neurons.

00:02:08.620 --> 00:02:10.919
The neurons are
the white circles.

00:02:10.919 --> 00:02:13.950
What a neuron does is
always the same thing.

00:02:13.950 --> 00:02:18.330
A neuron does a weighted
sum of all of its inputs,

00:02:18.330 --> 00:02:19.890
here the pixels.

00:02:19.890 --> 00:02:23.460
It adds another constant
that is called a bias.

00:02:23.460 --> 00:02:26.130
That's just an additional
degree of freedom.

00:02:26.130 --> 00:02:31.710
And then it will feed this sum
through an activation function.

00:02:31.710 --> 00:02:34.890
And that is just a function--
number in, transform,

00:02:34.890 --> 00:02:36.630
number out.

00:02:36.630 --> 00:02:39.030
We will see several of
those activation functions

00:02:39.030 --> 00:02:42.150
and the one thing they have
in common in neural networks

00:02:42.150 --> 00:02:45.600
is that they are non-linear.

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:46.890
So why 10 neurons?

00:02:46.890 --> 00:02:49.680
Well, simply because we are
classifying those digits

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:51.850
in 10 categories.

00:02:51.850 --> 00:02:54.290
We are trying to recognize
a zero, a one, a two,

00:02:54.290 --> 00:02:55.296
on to the nine.

00:02:55.296 --> 00:02:59.760
So what we are hoping for here
is that one of those neurons

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:03.660
will light up and tell us,
with a very strong output,

00:03:03.660 --> 00:03:06.810
that I have recognized
here an eight.

00:03:06.810 --> 00:03:07.740
All right.

00:03:07.740 --> 00:03:11.160
And for that, since this is
a classification problem,

00:03:11.160 --> 00:03:13.470
we are going to use a
very specific activation

00:03:13.470 --> 00:03:17.790
function, one that,
well, researchers

00:03:17.790 --> 00:03:20.780
tell us works really well
on classification problems.

00:03:20.780 --> 00:03:24.450
It's called softmax
and it's simply

00:03:24.450 --> 00:03:26.580
an exponential normalized.

00:03:26.580 --> 00:03:30.060
So what you do is that you
make all those weighted sums,

00:03:30.060 --> 00:03:32.390
then you elevate that
to the exponential.

00:03:32.390 --> 00:03:35.100
And once you have
your 10 exponentials,

00:03:35.100 --> 00:03:37.350
you compute the
norm of this vector

00:03:37.350 --> 00:03:39.840
and divide it by its
norm so that you get

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:41.910
values between zero and one.

00:03:41.910 --> 00:03:45.240
And those values, you will
be able to interpret them

00:03:45.240 --> 00:03:49.980
as probabilities, probabilities
of this being an eight, a one,

00:03:49.980 --> 00:03:52.290
or something else.

00:03:52.290 --> 00:03:55.250
You will be asking which norm?

00:03:55.250 --> 00:03:57.240
Any norm, doesn't matter--

00:03:57.240 --> 00:03:58.950
the length of the vector.

00:03:58.950 --> 00:04:02.770
You pick your favorite norm.

00:04:02.770 --> 00:04:04.500
There are several.

00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:07.860
Usually, for softmax,
we use L1, but L2

00:04:07.860 --> 00:04:11.290
which is the Euclidean normal
would work just as well.

00:04:11.290 --> 00:04:13.620
So what does
softmax do actually?

00:04:13.620 --> 00:04:16.470
You see, it's an exponential so
it's a very steeply increasing

00:04:16.470 --> 00:04:17.370
function.

00:04:17.370 --> 00:04:22.079
It will pull the data apart,
increase the differences,

00:04:22.079 --> 00:04:24.960
and when you divide all of
that, when you normalize

00:04:24.960 --> 00:04:28.800
the whole vector, you usually
end up with one of the values

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:31.170
being very close to one
and all the other values

00:04:31.170 --> 00:04:33.060
being very close to zero.

00:04:33.060 --> 00:04:36.870
So it's a way of pulling
the winner out on top

00:04:36.870 --> 00:04:40.691
without actually
destroying the information.

00:04:40.691 --> 00:04:41.190
All right.

00:04:41.190 --> 00:04:47.070
So now we need to formalize
this using a matrix multiply.

00:04:47.070 --> 00:04:51.130
I will remind you of what
a matrix multiply is,

00:04:51.130 --> 00:04:53.340
but we will do it
not one image, we

00:04:53.340 --> 00:04:57.900
are going to do this for a
batch of 100 images at a time.

00:04:57.900 --> 00:04:59.670
So what we have
here in my matrix

00:04:59.670 --> 00:05:03.830
is 100 images, one
image per line.

00:05:03.830 --> 00:05:08.440
The images are flattened,
all the pixels on one line.

00:05:08.440 --> 00:05:11.350
So I take my matrix of
weights, for the time being,

00:05:11.350 --> 00:05:12.850
I don't know what
these weights are,

00:05:12.850 --> 00:05:15.540
it's just weights so
I'm doing weighted sums.

00:05:15.540 --> 00:05:18.460
And I start the
matrix multiplication.

00:05:18.460 --> 00:05:22.460
So I do a weighted sum of all
the pixels of the first image.

00:05:22.460 --> 00:05:24.120
Here it is.

00:05:24.120 --> 00:05:26.280
And then if I continue
this matrix multiply

00:05:26.280 --> 00:05:28.710
using the second
column of weights,

00:05:28.710 --> 00:05:30.690
I get a weighted sum
of all the pixels

00:05:30.690 --> 00:05:33.210
of the first image for
the second neuron and then

00:05:33.210 --> 00:05:37.410
for the third neuron and
the fourth and so on.

00:05:37.410 --> 00:05:41.370
What is left is
to add the bias's,

00:05:41.370 --> 00:05:42.790
just an additional constant.

00:05:42.790 --> 00:05:45.750
Again, we don't know what
it is for the time being.

00:05:45.750 --> 00:05:48.060
And there is one
bias per neuron,

00:05:48.060 --> 00:05:50.460
that's why we have 10 biases.

00:05:50.460 --> 00:05:53.640
And now if I continue
this matrix multiply,

00:05:53.640 --> 00:05:56.730
I'm going to obtain
these weighted sums

00:05:56.730 --> 00:05:59.550
for the second image,
and the third image,

00:05:59.550 --> 00:06:02.910
and so on, until I have
processed all my images.

00:06:05.710 --> 00:06:09.750
I would like to write this
as a simple formula there.

00:06:09.750 --> 00:06:13.290
You see there is a
problem, x times w,

00:06:13.290 --> 00:06:17.940
you know that's a matrix of
10 columns by 100 images,

00:06:17.940 --> 00:06:20.200
and I have only 10 biases.

00:06:20.200 --> 00:06:22.850
I can't simply
add them together.

00:06:22.850 --> 00:06:24.080
Well, never mind.

00:06:24.080 --> 00:06:28.800
We will redefine
addition and it's OK

00:06:28.800 --> 00:06:31.900
if everybody accepts it.

00:06:31.900 --> 00:06:33.820
And actually, people
have already accepted it.

00:06:33.820 --> 00:06:35.850
It's called a broadcasting
add and that's

00:06:35.850 --> 00:06:37.680
the way you do
additions in NumPy,

00:06:37.680 --> 00:06:42.090
for instance, which is the
numerical library for Python.

00:06:42.090 --> 00:06:45.660
The way a broadcasting
add works is

00:06:45.660 --> 00:06:49.440
that if you're trying to add two
things which don't match, not

00:06:49.440 --> 00:06:52.050
the same dimensions, you
can't do the addition,

00:06:52.050 --> 00:06:55.500
you try to replicate
the small one as much

00:06:55.500 --> 00:06:58.350
as needed to make
the sizes match

00:06:58.350 --> 00:07:00.636
and then you do the addition.

00:07:00.636 --> 00:07:02.260
That's exactly what
we need to do here.

00:07:02.260 --> 00:07:04.050
We have only those 10 biases.

00:07:04.050 --> 00:07:07.260
So it's the same biases
on all the lines.

00:07:07.260 --> 00:07:10.890
We just need to replicate this
bias vector on all the lines,

00:07:10.890 --> 00:07:14.070
and that's exactly what this
generalized broadcasting

00:07:14.070 --> 00:07:15.270
add does.

00:07:15.270 --> 00:07:18.630
So we will just
write it as a plus.

00:07:18.630 --> 00:07:21.920
And this is where
I wanted to get to.

00:07:21.920 --> 00:07:25.950
I want you to remember this
as the formula describing

00:07:25.950 --> 00:07:29.770
one layer in a neural network.

00:07:29.770 --> 00:07:31.620
So let's go through this again.

00:07:31.620 --> 00:07:35.250
In x, we have a batch
of images, 100 images,

00:07:35.250 --> 00:07:37.350
all the pixels on one line.

00:07:37.350 --> 00:07:40.410
In w, we have all of our
weights for the 10 neurons,

00:07:40.410 --> 00:07:43.920
all the weights in the system.

00:07:43.920 --> 00:07:46.950
x times w are all
about weighted sums.

00:07:46.950 --> 00:07:49.800
We add the biases,
and then we feed this

00:07:49.800 --> 00:07:54.690
through our activation function,
in this case softmax, the way

00:07:54.690 --> 00:07:56.700
it works is lined by line.

00:07:56.700 --> 00:07:58.680
Line by line, we
take the 10 values,

00:07:58.680 --> 00:08:02.190
elevate them to the
exponential, normalize the line.

00:08:02.190 --> 00:08:04.620
Next line, 10 values, elevate
them to the exponential,

00:08:04.620 --> 00:08:06.810
normalize the line, and so on.

00:08:06.810 --> 00:08:13.380
So what we get in the output
is, for each image, 10 values

00:08:13.380 --> 00:08:19.690
which look like probabilities
and which are our predictions.

00:08:19.690 --> 00:08:21.510
So, of course, we
still don't know

00:08:21.510 --> 00:08:24.000
what those weights
and biases are

00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:26.950
and that's where the trick
is in neural networks.

00:08:26.950 --> 00:08:30.930
We are going to train
this neural network

00:08:30.930 --> 00:08:33.900
to actually figure out
the correct weights

00:08:33.900 --> 00:08:35.130
and biases by itself.

00:08:38.190 --> 00:08:41.130
Well, this is how we
write this in TensorFlow.

00:08:41.130 --> 00:08:44.431
You see, not very different.

00:08:44.431 --> 00:08:44.930
OK.

00:08:44.930 --> 00:08:48.660
TensorFlow has this in n
library for neural network

00:08:48.660 --> 00:08:50.760
which has all sorts of
very useful functions

00:08:50.760 --> 00:08:54.580
for neural networks, for
example, softmax and so on.

00:08:54.580 --> 00:08:58.860
So let's go train.

00:08:58.860 --> 00:09:00.780
When you train,
you've got images,

00:09:00.780 --> 00:09:03.600
but you know what
those images are.

00:09:03.600 --> 00:09:07.260
So your network, you initialize
your weights and biases

00:09:07.260 --> 00:09:12.660
at random value and your network
will output some probability.

00:09:12.660 --> 00:09:16.230
Since you know
what this image is,

00:09:16.230 --> 00:09:21.510
you can tell it that it's
not this, it should be that.

00:09:21.510 --> 00:09:25.800
So that is called a
one-hot encoded vector.

00:09:25.800 --> 00:09:30.660
It's a not very fancy
way of encoding numbers.

00:09:30.660 --> 00:09:32.970
Basically, here are our
numbers from zero to nine.

00:09:32.970 --> 00:09:37.260
We encode them as 10 bits, all
at zero and just one of them

00:09:37.260 --> 00:09:40.566
is a one at the index of the
number we want to encode.

00:09:40.566 --> 00:09:42.510
Here are six.

00:09:42.510 --> 00:09:43.010
Why?

00:09:43.010 --> 00:09:46.710
Well, because then, it's in the
same shape as our predictions

00:09:46.710 --> 00:09:51.180
and we can compute a
distance between those two.

00:09:51.180 --> 00:09:55.440
So again, many ways of
computing distances.

00:09:55.440 --> 00:09:58.410
The Euclidean distance,
the normal distance, sum

00:09:58.410 --> 00:10:02.820
of differences squared
would work, not a problem.

00:10:02.820 --> 00:10:06.990
But scientists tell us that
for classification problems,

00:10:06.990 --> 00:10:10.380
this distance, the cross
entropy, works slightly better.

00:10:10.380 --> 00:10:11.760
So we'll use this one.

00:10:11.760 --> 00:10:13.210
How does it work?

00:10:13.210 --> 00:10:16.500
It's the sum across the
vectors of the values

00:10:16.500 --> 00:10:20.460
on the top multiplied by
the logarithms of the values

00:10:20.460 --> 00:10:22.869
on the bottom, and then
we add in minus sign

00:10:22.869 --> 00:10:25.160
because all the values on
the bottom are less than one,

00:10:25.160 --> 00:10:27.570
so all the logarithms
are negative.

00:10:27.570 --> 00:10:29.460
So that's the distance.

00:10:29.460 --> 00:10:33.660
And of course, we
will tell the system

00:10:33.660 --> 00:10:37.500
to minimize the distance between
what it thinks is the truth

00:10:37.500 --> 00:10:39.520
and what we know to be true.

00:10:39.520 --> 00:10:41.910
So this we will call
our error function

00:10:41.910 --> 00:10:45.150
and the training will
be guided by an effort

00:10:45.150 --> 00:10:47.144
to minimize the error function.

00:10:47.144 --> 00:10:48.810
So let's see how this
works in practice.

00:10:56.370 --> 00:10:59.120
So in this little
visualization, I'm

00:10:59.120 --> 00:11:03.180
showing you over there,
my training images.

00:11:03.180 --> 00:11:08.540
You see it's training so you
see this batches of 100 training

00:11:08.540 --> 00:11:11.177
images being fed
into the system.

00:11:11.177 --> 00:11:13.010
On the white background,
you have the images

00:11:13.010 --> 00:11:16.940
that have been already correctly
recognized by the system.

00:11:16.940 --> 00:11:22.130
On a red background, images
that are still missed.

00:11:22.130 --> 00:11:24.710
So then, on the
middle graph, you

00:11:24.710 --> 00:11:30.240
see our error function, computed
both on the training dataset

00:11:30.240 --> 00:11:37.130
and we also kept aside a set of
images which we have never seen

00:11:37.130 --> 00:11:39.260
during training for testing.

00:11:39.260 --> 00:11:41.840
Of course, if you want
to test the real world

00:11:41.840 --> 00:11:43.940
performance of your
neural network,

00:11:43.940 --> 00:11:46.790
you have to do this on a set
of images which you have never

00:11:46.790 --> 00:11:48.810
seen during training.

00:11:48.810 --> 00:11:52.600
So here we have
60,000 training images

00:11:52.600 --> 00:11:56.180
and I set aside 10,000
test images which you see

00:11:56.180 --> 00:11:57.520
in the bottom graph over there.

00:11:57.520 --> 00:11:59.870
They are a bit small.

00:11:59.870 --> 00:12:04.070
You see only 1,000 of them here.

00:12:04.070 --> 00:12:08.820
So imagine, there are nine more
screens of pictures like that.

00:12:08.820 --> 00:12:13.340
But I sorted all the badly
recognized one at the top.

00:12:13.340 --> 00:12:16.130
So you see all the ones that
have been badly recognized

00:12:16.130 --> 00:12:20.360
and below are nine screens of
correctly recognized images,

00:12:20.360 --> 00:12:23.510
here after 2,000
rounds of training.

00:12:23.510 --> 00:12:26.540
So there is a little
scale on the side here.

00:12:26.540 --> 00:12:30.020
It shows you that it's
already capable of recognizing

00:12:30.020 --> 00:12:34.550
92% of our images with this very
simple model, just 10 neuron's,

00:12:34.550 --> 00:12:35.210
nothing else.

00:12:39.260 --> 00:12:42.481
And that's what you get on the
top graph, the accuracy graph,

00:12:42.481 --> 00:12:42.980
as well.

00:12:42.980 --> 00:12:46.760
That's simply the percentage
of correctly recognized images,

00:12:46.760 --> 00:12:50.630
both on test and training data.

00:12:50.630 --> 00:12:52.370
So what else do we have?

00:12:52.370 --> 00:12:56.460
We have our weights and biases,
those two diagrams are simply

00:12:56.460 --> 00:12:59.060
percentiles, so it
shows you the spread

00:12:59.060 --> 00:13:01.220
of all the weights and biases.

00:13:01.220 --> 00:13:05.390
And that's just useful to
see that they are moving.

00:13:05.390 --> 00:13:11.000
They both started at zero
and they took some values

00:13:11.000 --> 00:13:12.770
for the weights between
one and minus one

00:13:12.770 --> 00:13:15.770
for biasses between
two and minus two.

00:13:15.770 --> 00:13:17.990
It's helpful to keep an
eye on those diagrams

00:13:17.990 --> 00:13:21.680
and see that we are not
diverging completely.

00:13:21.680 --> 00:13:24.490
So that's the
training algorithm.

00:13:24.490 --> 00:13:27.620
You give it training images,
it gives you a prediction,

00:13:27.620 --> 00:13:29.810
you compute the distance
between the prediction

00:13:29.810 --> 00:13:31.560
and what you know to be true.

00:13:31.560 --> 00:13:33.320
You use that
distance as an error

00:13:33.320 --> 00:13:38.540
function to guide a mechanism
that will drive the error down

00:13:38.540 --> 00:13:39.895
by modifying weights and biases.

00:13:45.430 --> 00:13:52.690
So now let's write
this in TensorFlow.

00:13:52.690 --> 00:13:55.690
And I'll get more
explicit about exactly how

00:13:55.690 --> 00:13:58.750
this training works.

00:13:58.750 --> 00:14:01.850
So we need to write
this in TensorFlow.

00:14:01.850 --> 00:14:03.910
The first thing you
do in TensorFlow

00:14:03.910 --> 00:14:07.480
is define variables
and placeholders.

00:14:07.480 --> 00:14:11.050
A variable is a
degree of freedom

00:14:11.050 --> 00:14:15.670
of our system, something we are
asking TensorFlow to compute

00:14:15.670 --> 00:14:18.100
for us through training.

00:14:18.100 --> 00:14:20.945
So in our case, those are
our weights and biases.

00:14:23.450 --> 00:14:28.430
And we will need to
feed in training data.

00:14:28.430 --> 00:14:32.210
So for this data that will
be fed in at training time,

00:14:32.210 --> 00:14:34.330
we define a placeholder.

00:14:34.330 --> 00:14:42.960
You see here x is a placeholder
for our training images.

00:14:42.960 --> 00:14:45.230
Let's look at the
shape in brackets.

00:14:45.230 --> 00:14:49.170
What you have is the shape of
this multidimensional matrix,

00:14:49.170 --> 00:14:51.110
which we call a tensor.

00:14:51.110 --> 00:14:53.720
So the first dimension is none.

00:14:53.720 --> 00:14:56.480
It says I don't know yet so this
will be the number of images

00:14:56.480 --> 00:14:57.830
in a batch.

00:14:57.830 --> 00:15:00.790
This will be determined
at training time.

00:15:00.790 --> 00:15:04.100
If we give 100 images,
this will be 100.

00:15:04.100 --> 00:15:07.710
Then 28 by 28 is the
size of our images

00:15:07.710 --> 00:15:10.670
and one is the number
of values per pixel.

00:15:10.670 --> 00:15:12.760
So that's not useful
at all because we

00:15:12.760 --> 00:15:14.060
are handling grayscale images.

00:15:14.060 --> 00:15:15.050
I just put it there.

00:15:15.050 --> 00:15:18.380
In case you wanted to handle
color images, that would

00:15:18.380 --> 00:15:22.160
be three values per pixel.

00:15:22.160 --> 00:15:22.970
So OK.

00:15:22.970 --> 00:15:28.620
We have our placeholders,
we have our variables,

00:15:28.620 --> 00:15:31.850
now we are ready
to write our model.

00:15:31.850 --> 00:15:35.330
So that line you see on
the top is our model.

00:15:35.330 --> 00:15:38.390
It's what we have determined
to be the line representing

00:15:38.390 --> 00:15:40.820
one layer of a neural network.

00:15:40.820 --> 00:15:44.620
The only change is
that reshape operation.

00:15:44.620 --> 00:15:47.740
You remember, our images,
they come in as 28

00:15:47.740 --> 00:15:50.900
by 28 pixel images and
we want to flatten them

00:15:50.900 --> 00:15:53.610
as one big vector of pixels.

00:15:53.610 --> 00:15:55.600
So that's what reshape does.

00:15:55.600 --> 00:15:58.680
784 is 28 by 28.

00:15:58.680 --> 00:16:00.801
It's all the pixels in one line.

00:16:00.801 --> 00:16:01.300
All right.

00:16:01.300 --> 00:16:04.610
I need a second placeholder
for the known answers,

00:16:04.610 --> 00:16:06.500
the labels of my
training images,

00:16:06.500 --> 00:16:09.050
labels like this is a one, this
is a zero, this is a seven,

00:16:09.050 --> 00:16:10.700
this is a five.

00:16:10.700 --> 00:16:13.305
And now that I have my
predictions and my known

00:16:13.305 --> 00:16:17.900
labels, I'm ready to compute
my error function, which

00:16:17.900 --> 00:16:22.480
is the cross entropy using
the formula we've seen before.

00:16:22.480 --> 00:16:28.040
So the sum across the vector
of the elements of the labels

00:16:28.040 --> 00:16:32.750
multiplied by elements of the
logarithm of the predictions.

00:16:32.750 --> 00:16:34.550
So now I have my error function.

00:16:34.550 --> 00:16:36.230
What do I do with it?

00:16:36.230 --> 00:16:39.140
What you have on the bottom,
I won't go into that.

00:16:39.140 --> 00:16:42.020
That is simply the
computation of the percentage

00:16:42.020 --> 00:16:45.110
of correctly recognized images.

00:16:45.110 --> 00:16:47.011
You can skip that.

00:16:47.011 --> 00:16:47.510
OK.

00:16:47.510 --> 00:16:51.070
Now we get to the
actual heart of what

00:16:51.070 --> 00:16:53.250
TensorFlow will do for you.

00:16:53.250 --> 00:16:54.950
So we have our error function.

00:16:54.950 --> 00:16:56.930
We pick an optimizer.

00:16:56.930 --> 00:16:59.340
There is a full library of them.

00:16:59.340 --> 00:17:01.400
They have different
characteristics.

00:17:01.400 --> 00:17:07.770
And we ask the optimizer to
minimize our error function.

00:17:07.770 --> 00:17:10.849
So what is this going to do?

00:17:10.849 --> 00:17:14.420
When you do this, TensorFlow
takes your error function

00:17:14.420 --> 00:17:16.960
and computes the partial
derivatives of that error

00:17:16.960 --> 00:17:21.109
function relatively to all
the weights and all the biases

00:17:21.109 --> 00:17:22.700
in the system.

00:17:22.700 --> 00:17:24.740
That's a big vector
because there are lots

00:17:24.740 --> 00:17:26.579
of weights and lots of biases.

00:17:26.579 --> 00:17:27.109
How many?

00:17:30.500 --> 00:17:41.400
w, the weights, is a variable
of almost 8,000 values.

00:17:41.400 --> 00:17:45.030
So this vector we
get mathematically

00:17:45.030 --> 00:17:47.760
is called a gradient.

00:17:47.760 --> 00:17:52.214
And the gradient has
one nice property.

00:17:52.214 --> 00:17:54.380
Who knows what is the nice
property of the gradient?

00:17:54.380 --> 00:17:57.980
It points-- Yeah.

00:18:01.211 --> 00:18:01.710
Almost.

00:18:01.710 --> 00:18:05.670
It points up, we had a minus
sign, it points down, exactly.

00:18:05.670 --> 00:18:07.110
Down in which space?

00:18:07.110 --> 00:18:10.326
We are in the space of all the
weight and all the variables

00:18:10.326 --> 00:18:11.700
and the function
we are computing

00:18:11.700 --> 00:18:13.750
is our error function.

00:18:13.750 --> 00:18:15.750
So when we say
down in this space,

00:18:15.750 --> 00:18:21.060
it means it gives us a direction
in the space of weights

00:18:21.060 --> 00:18:25.170
and biases into which to
go to modify our weights

00:18:25.170 --> 00:18:29.640
and biases in order to make
our error function smaller.

00:18:29.640 --> 00:18:31.530
So that is the training.

00:18:31.530 --> 00:18:34.320
You compute this gradient
and it gives you an arrow.

00:18:34.320 --> 00:18:36.680
You take a little
step along this arrow.

00:18:36.680 --> 00:18:39.070
Well, you are in the space
of weights and biases,

00:18:39.070 --> 00:18:41.460
so taking a little step
means you modify your weights

00:18:41.460 --> 00:18:45.780
and biases by this little delta,
and you get into a location

00:18:45.780 --> 00:18:48.182
where the error is now smaller.

00:18:48.182 --> 00:18:49.140
Well, that's fantastic.

00:18:49.140 --> 00:18:50.348
That's exactly what you want.

00:18:50.348 --> 00:18:53.220
Then you repeat this
using a second batch

00:18:53.220 --> 00:18:54.760
of training images.

00:18:54.760 --> 00:18:59.370
And again, using a third batch
of training images, and so on.

00:18:59.370 --> 00:19:01.590
So it's called gradient
descent because you follow

00:19:01.590 --> 00:19:05.250
the gradient to head down.

00:19:05.250 --> 00:19:09.540
And so we are ready to
write our training loop.

00:19:09.540 --> 00:19:11.430
There is one more
thing I need to explain

00:19:11.430 --> 00:19:14.350
to you about TensorFlow.

00:19:14.350 --> 00:19:17.250
TensorFlow has a
deferred execution model.

00:19:17.250 --> 00:19:20.780
So everything we
wrote up to now,

00:19:20.780 --> 00:19:26.280
all the tf dot
something here commands,

00:19:26.280 --> 00:19:28.730
does not actually--
when that is executed,

00:19:28.730 --> 00:19:31.470
it doesn't produce values.

00:19:31.470 --> 00:19:35.460
It builds a graph, a
computation graph, in memory.

00:19:35.460 --> 00:19:36.640
Why is that important?

00:19:36.640 --> 00:19:44.090
Well, first of all, this
derivation trick here,

00:19:44.090 --> 00:19:46.100
the computation of
the gradient, that

00:19:46.100 --> 00:19:48.530
is actually a formal derivation.

00:19:48.530 --> 00:19:50.900
TensorFlow takes
the formula that you

00:19:50.900 --> 00:19:52.670
give it to define
your error function

00:19:52.670 --> 00:19:54.800
and does a formal
derivation on it.

00:19:54.800 --> 00:20:00.140
So it needs to know the full
graph of how you computed this

00:20:00.140 --> 00:20:02.240
to do this formal derivation.

00:20:02.240 --> 00:20:04.640
And the second thing it
will use this graph for

00:20:04.640 --> 00:20:08.430
is that TensorFlow is built
for distributed computing.

00:20:08.430 --> 00:20:10.730
And there, as well,
to distribute a graph

00:20:10.730 --> 00:20:14.870
on multiple machine, it helps
to know what the graph is.

00:20:14.870 --> 00:20:16.950
OK.

00:20:16.950 --> 00:20:20.390
So this is all very
useful, but it means for us

00:20:20.390 --> 00:20:22.850
that we have to go
through an additional loop

00:20:22.850 --> 00:20:26.900
to actually get values
from our computations.

00:20:26.900 --> 00:20:28.430
The way you do
this in TensorFlow

00:20:28.430 --> 00:20:32.300
is that you define a session
and then in the session,

00:20:32.300 --> 00:20:38.090
you call sess.run on one edge
of your computation graph.

00:20:40.940 --> 00:20:43.130
That will give
you actual values,

00:20:43.130 --> 00:20:45.350
but of course, for
this to work, you

00:20:45.350 --> 00:20:48.020
have to fill in all
the placeholders

00:20:48.020 --> 00:20:51.570
that you have defined
now with real values.

00:20:51.570 --> 00:20:56.210
So for this to work, I will need
to fill in the training images

00:20:56.210 --> 00:20:58.040
and the training
labels for which

00:20:58.040 --> 00:21:00.320
I have defined placeholders.

00:21:00.320 --> 00:21:04.880
And the syntax is simply the
train_data dictionary there.

00:21:04.880 --> 00:21:08.780
You see the keys of the
dictionary, x and y underscore,

00:21:08.780 --> 00:21:12.170
are the placeholders
that I have defined.

00:21:12.170 --> 00:21:14.880
And then I can sess.run
on my training step.

00:21:14.880 --> 00:21:17.660
I pass in this training
data and that is

00:21:17.660 --> 00:21:19.950
where the actual magic happens.

00:21:19.950 --> 00:21:23.510
Just a reminder, what
is this training step?

00:21:23.510 --> 00:21:28.460
Well it's what you got when you
asked the optimizer to minimize

00:21:28.460 --> 00:21:30.090
your error function.

00:21:30.090 --> 00:21:32.930
So the training
step, when executed,

00:21:32.930 --> 00:21:36.110
is actually what computes
this gradient using

00:21:36.110 --> 00:21:39.500
the current batch of images,
training images and labels,

00:21:39.500 --> 00:21:44.360
and follows it a little to
modify the weights and biases

00:21:44.360 --> 00:21:47.480
and end up with better
weights and biases.

00:21:47.480 --> 00:21:48.990
I said a little.

00:21:48.990 --> 00:21:50.810
I come back to this.

00:21:50.810 --> 00:21:54.560
What is that learning
rate over there?

00:21:54.560 --> 00:22:01.070
Well, I can't make a big
step along the gradient.

00:22:01.070 --> 00:22:02.540
Why not?

00:22:02.540 --> 00:22:06.200
Imagine you're in the mountains,
you know where down is.

00:22:06.200 --> 00:22:07.460
We have senses for that.

00:22:07.460 --> 00:22:08.840
We don't know to
derive anything.

00:22:08.840 --> 00:22:10.550
We know where down is.

00:22:10.550 --> 00:22:13.160
And you want to reach
the bottom of the valley.

00:22:13.160 --> 00:22:18.410
Now, if every step you
make is a 10 mile step,

00:22:18.410 --> 00:22:21.050
you will probably be jumping
from one side of the valley

00:22:21.050 --> 00:22:24.050
to the other without
ever reaching the bottom.

00:22:24.050 --> 00:22:25.850
So if you want to
reach the bottom,

00:22:25.850 --> 00:22:27.530
even if you know
where a down is,

00:22:27.530 --> 00:22:31.470
you have to make small
steps in that direction,

00:22:31.470 --> 00:22:33.200
and then you will
reach the bottom.

00:22:33.200 --> 00:22:36.690
So the same here, when
we compute this gradient,

00:22:36.690 --> 00:22:42.800
we multiplied by this very small
value so as to take small steps

00:22:42.800 --> 00:22:45.650
and be sure that we not jumping
from one side of the valley

00:22:45.650 --> 00:22:47.820
to the other.

00:22:47.820 --> 00:22:48.320
All right.

00:22:48.320 --> 00:22:50.000
So let's finish our training.

00:22:50.000 --> 00:22:55.520
Basically, in a loop, we load
a batch of 100 training images

00:22:55.520 --> 00:22:57.080
and labels.

00:22:57.080 --> 00:23:02.690
We run this training step which
adjusts our weights and biases.

00:23:02.690 --> 00:23:05.300
And we repeat.

00:23:05.300 --> 00:23:09.340
All the rest of the stuff on the
bottom, it's just for display.

00:23:09.340 --> 00:23:12.710
I'm computing the accuracy
and the cross entropy

00:23:12.710 --> 00:23:16.670
on my training data and
again, on my test data

00:23:16.670 --> 00:23:21.140
so that I can show you
four curves over there.

00:23:21.140 --> 00:23:22.524
It is just for display.

00:23:22.524 --> 00:23:24.440
It has nothing to do
with the training itself.

00:23:27.050 --> 00:23:28.520
All right.

00:23:28.520 --> 00:23:29.510
So that was it.

00:23:29.510 --> 00:23:33.240
That's the entire code
here on one slide.

00:23:33.240 --> 00:23:36.530
Let's go through this again.

00:23:36.530 --> 00:23:40.610
At the beginning, you define
variables for everything

00:23:40.610 --> 00:23:43.550
that you want TensorFlow
to compute for you.

00:23:43.550 --> 00:23:46.460
So here are our
weights and biasses.

00:23:46.460 --> 00:23:48.890
You define placeholders
for everything

00:23:48.890 --> 00:23:54.200
that you will be feeding during
the training, namely our images

00:23:54.200 --> 00:23:56.900
and our training labels.

00:23:56.900 --> 00:23:59.060
Then you define your model.

00:23:59.060 --> 00:24:01.160
Your model gives
you predictions.

00:24:01.160 --> 00:24:02.810
You can compare
those predictions

00:24:02.810 --> 00:24:05.570
with your known labels,
compare the distance

00:24:05.570 --> 00:24:09.050
between the two, which is
the cross entropy here,

00:24:09.050 --> 00:24:11.940
and use that as
an error function.

00:24:11.940 --> 00:24:16.340
So you pick an optimizer and you
ask the optimizer to minimize

00:24:16.340 --> 00:24:18.170
your error function.

00:24:18.170 --> 00:24:19.880
That gives all the
gradients and all

00:24:19.880 --> 00:24:22.390
that, it gives you
a training step.

00:24:22.390 --> 00:24:26.380
And now, in a loop, you
load a batch of images.

00:24:26.380 --> 00:24:27.710
You're on your training step.

00:24:27.710 --> 00:24:29.330
You load a batch of
images and labels,

00:24:29.330 --> 00:24:32.750
you run your training step,
and you do this in a loop,

00:24:32.750 --> 00:24:37.220
and hoping this will
converge, and usually it does.

00:24:37.220 --> 00:24:41.450
You see here, it did converge
and with this approach,

00:24:41.450 --> 00:24:46.820
we got 92% accuracy.

00:24:46.820 --> 00:24:50.980
Small recap of all the
ingredients we put in our pot

00:24:50.980 --> 00:24:52.510
so far.

00:24:52.510 --> 00:24:55.090
We have a softmax
activation function.

00:24:55.090 --> 00:24:58.150
We have the cross entropy
as an error function.

00:24:58.150 --> 00:25:00.040
And we did this
mini batching thing

00:25:00.040 --> 00:25:03.970
where we train on 100 images
at a time, do one step,

00:25:03.970 --> 00:25:08.470
and then load another
batch of images.

00:25:08.470 --> 00:25:14.350
So is 92% accuracy good?

00:25:14.350 --> 00:25:16.689
No, it's horrible.

00:25:16.689 --> 00:25:18.730
Imagine you're actually
using this in production.

00:25:18.730 --> 00:25:22.030
I don't know, in the post
office, your decoding zip

00:25:22.030 --> 00:25:23.770
codes.

00:25:23.770 --> 00:25:29.580
92% out of 100 digits,
you have eight bad values?

00:25:29.580 --> 00:25:31.810
No, not usable in production.

00:25:31.810 --> 00:25:33.160
Forget it.

00:25:33.160 --> 00:25:34.340
So how do we fix it?

00:25:34.340 --> 00:25:36.200
Well deep learning.

00:25:36.200 --> 00:25:37.060
We'll go deep.

00:25:37.060 --> 00:25:39.730
We can just stack those layers.

00:25:39.730 --> 00:25:40.550
How do we do that?

00:25:40.550 --> 00:25:42.730
Well, it's very simple.

00:25:42.730 --> 00:25:45.010
Look at the top
layer of neurons.

00:25:45.010 --> 00:25:46.690
It does what we just did.

00:25:46.690 --> 00:25:51.550
It computes weighted
sums of pixels.

00:25:51.550 --> 00:25:54.370
But we can just as
easily add a second layer

00:25:54.370 --> 00:25:57.670
of neurons that will
compute weighted sums all

00:25:57.670 --> 00:26:00.070
the outputs of the first layer.

00:26:00.070 --> 00:26:02.800
And that's how you
stack layers to produce

00:26:02.800 --> 00:26:06.520
a deep neural network.

00:26:06.520 --> 00:26:08.860
Now we are going to change
our activation function.

00:26:08.860 --> 00:26:11.980
We keep softmax for
the output layer

00:26:11.980 --> 00:26:14.260
because softmax has
these nice properties

00:26:14.260 --> 00:26:17.650
of pulling a winner
apart and producing

00:26:17.650 --> 00:26:20.600
numbers between zero and one.

00:26:20.600 --> 00:26:25.810
But for the rest, we use a very
classical activation function.

00:26:25.810 --> 00:26:28.540
In neural networks,
it's called the sigmoid,

00:26:28.540 --> 00:26:32.890
and it's basically, the simplest
possible continuous function

00:26:32.890 --> 00:26:35.040
that goes from zero to one.

00:26:35.040 --> 00:26:36.561
OK.

00:26:36.561 --> 00:26:37.060
All right.

00:26:37.060 --> 00:26:39.010
Let's write this model.

00:26:39.010 --> 00:26:45.490
So we have now one set of
weights and one set of biasses

00:26:45.490 --> 00:26:47.170
per layer.

00:26:47.170 --> 00:26:50.890
That's why we use C5 pairs here.

00:26:50.890 --> 00:26:55.060
And our model will actually
look very familiar to you.

00:26:55.060 --> 00:26:56.410
Look at the first line.

00:26:56.410 --> 00:26:59.530
It's exactly what we
have seen before for one

00:26:59.530 --> 00:27:01.930
layer of a neural network.

00:27:01.930 --> 00:27:05.080
Now what we do with
the output, Y1,

00:27:05.080 --> 00:27:09.100
is that we use it as the
input in the second line,

00:27:09.100 --> 00:27:11.500
and so on, we chain those.

00:27:11.500 --> 00:27:13.930
It's just that on the
last line, the activation

00:27:13.930 --> 00:27:17.140
function we use is the softmax.

00:27:17.140 --> 00:27:20.830
So that's all the
changes we did.

00:27:20.830 --> 00:27:24.820
And we can try to
run this again.

00:27:24.820 --> 00:27:27.695
So oops.

00:27:33.030 --> 00:27:36.140
This one.

00:27:36.140 --> 00:27:39.002
Run.

00:27:39.002 --> 00:27:41.424
Run.

00:27:41.424 --> 00:27:43.860
Run.

00:27:43.860 --> 00:27:50.020
And it's coming.

00:27:50.020 --> 00:27:53.300
Well, I don't like
this slope here.

00:27:53.300 --> 00:27:55.330
It shouldn't be shooting
up really sharp.

00:27:55.330 --> 00:27:57.110
It's a bit slow.

00:27:57.110 --> 00:28:01.340
Actually, I have a
solution for that.

00:28:01.340 --> 00:28:05.200
I lied to you when I said that
the sigmoid was the most widely

00:28:05.200 --> 00:28:07.600
used activation function.

00:28:07.600 --> 00:28:11.080
That was true in the
past, and today, people

00:28:11.080 --> 00:28:14.170
invented a new activation
function, which is called

00:28:14.170 --> 00:28:16.670
the Relu, and this is a relu.

00:28:16.670 --> 00:28:18.710
It's even simpler.

00:28:18.710 --> 00:28:22.630
It's just zero for all
negative values and identity

00:28:22.630 --> 00:28:25.890
for all positive values.

00:28:25.890 --> 00:28:27.960
Now this actually works better.

00:28:27.960 --> 00:28:29.940
It has lots of advantages.

00:28:29.940 --> 00:28:33.330
Why does it work better?

00:28:33.330 --> 00:28:34.260
We don't know.

00:28:34.260 --> 00:28:36.662
People tried it,
it worked better.

00:28:36.662 --> 00:28:39.370
[LAUGHTER]

00:28:39.370 --> 00:28:40.670
I'm being honest here.

00:28:40.670 --> 00:28:43.090
If you had a researcher
here, he would

00:28:43.090 --> 00:28:45.580
fill your head with
equations and prove it,

00:28:45.580 --> 00:28:48.970
but he would have done those
equations after the fact.

00:28:48.970 --> 00:28:51.050
People already tried
it, it worked better.

00:28:51.050 --> 00:28:54.310
Actually, they got
inspiration from biology.

00:28:54.310 --> 00:28:56.440
It is said, I don't
know if it is true,

00:28:56.440 --> 00:29:00.340
but I heard that the
sigmoid was the preferred

00:29:00.340 --> 00:29:04.800
model of biologists for our
actual biological neurons

00:29:04.800 --> 00:29:09.340
and that today, biologist
thinks that neurons in our head

00:29:09.340 --> 00:29:12.040
work more like this.

00:29:12.040 --> 00:29:14.080
And the guys in
computer science got

00:29:14.080 --> 00:29:17.740
inspiration from that,
tried it, works better.

00:29:17.740 --> 00:29:18.400
How better?

00:29:18.400 --> 00:29:22.600
Well, this is just the
beginning of the training.

00:29:22.600 --> 00:29:26.440
This is what we get with our
sigmoids, just 300 iterations,

00:29:26.440 --> 00:29:28.570
so just the beginning.

00:29:28.570 --> 00:29:30.930
And this is what
we get from relus.

00:29:30.930 --> 00:29:32.710
Well, I prefer this.

00:29:32.710 --> 00:29:35.590
The accuracy shoots
up really sharp.

00:29:35.590 --> 00:29:37.990
The cross entropy goes
down really sharp.

00:29:37.990 --> 00:29:40.060
It's much faster.

00:29:40.060 --> 00:29:43.150
And actually, here on
this very simple problem,

00:29:43.150 --> 00:29:47.170
the sigmoid would have
recovered, it's not an issue,

00:29:47.170 --> 00:29:53.110
but in very deep networks,
sometimes with the sigmoid,

00:29:53.110 --> 00:29:54.820
you don't converge at all.

00:29:54.820 --> 00:29:58.600
And the relu solves that
problem to some extent.

00:29:58.600 --> 00:30:01.760
So the relu it is for
most of our issues.

00:30:01.760 --> 00:30:02.260
OK.

00:30:02.260 --> 00:30:03.940
So now let's train.

00:30:03.940 --> 00:30:08.140
Let's do this for 10,000
iterations, five layers,

00:30:08.140 --> 00:30:09.130
look at that.

00:30:09.130 --> 00:30:11.470
98% accuracy.

00:30:11.470 --> 00:30:13.420
First of all, oh, yeah.

00:30:13.420 --> 00:30:16.240
We went from 92 to 98
just by adding layers.

00:30:16.240 --> 00:30:18.130
That's fantastic.

00:30:18.130 --> 00:30:20.870
But look at those curves.

00:30:20.870 --> 00:30:22.150
They're all messy.

00:30:22.150 --> 00:30:24.040
What is all this noise?

00:30:24.040 --> 00:30:25.960
Well, when you see
noise like that,

00:30:25.960 --> 00:30:28.540
it means that you
are going too fast.

00:30:28.540 --> 00:30:30.820
You're actually jumping
from one side of the valley

00:30:30.820 --> 00:30:34.060
to the other, without
critically reaching the bottom

00:30:34.060 --> 00:30:36.500
of your error function.

00:30:36.500 --> 00:30:39.490
So we have a solution for
that, but it's not just

00:30:39.490 --> 00:30:46.180
to go slower, because then you
would spend 10 times more time

00:30:46.180 --> 00:30:48.130
training.

00:30:48.130 --> 00:30:52.100
The solution, actually,
is to start fast and then

00:30:52.100 --> 00:30:56.080
slow down as you train.

00:30:56.080 --> 00:30:57.490
It's called learning rate decay.

00:30:57.490 --> 00:31:02.030
We usually decay the learning
rates on an exponential curve.

00:31:02.030 --> 00:31:03.550
So yes, I hear you.

00:31:03.550 --> 00:31:06.730
It sounds very simple,
why this little

00:31:06.730 --> 00:31:11.620
trick, but let me play you
the video of what this does.

00:31:11.620 --> 00:31:13.220
It's actually quite spectacular.

00:31:16.890 --> 00:31:18.640
So it's almost there.

00:31:18.640 --> 00:31:23.400
Should I have the
end of it on a slide.

00:31:23.400 --> 00:31:24.340
Yeah, that's it.

00:31:24.340 --> 00:31:28.920
So this is what we had
using a fixed learning rate

00:31:28.920 --> 00:31:32.670
and just by switching to a
decaying learning rate, look,

00:31:32.670 --> 00:31:33.840
it's spectacular.

00:31:33.840 --> 00:31:36.030
All the noise is gone.

00:31:36.030 --> 00:31:37.110
And for the first--

00:31:37.110 --> 00:31:38.680
just with this little trick--

00:31:38.680 --> 00:31:40.485
really, this is
not rocket science,

00:31:40.485 --> 00:31:43.280
it's just going slightly
slower towards the end

00:31:43.280 --> 00:31:44.530
and all the noise is gone.

00:31:44.530 --> 00:31:49.440
And look at the blue curve,
the training accuracy curve.

00:31:49.440 --> 00:31:52.950
Towards the end,
it's stuck at 100%.

00:31:52.950 --> 00:31:56.550
So here, for the first time,
we built a neural network

00:31:56.550 --> 00:32:02.770
that was capable of learning all
of our training set perfectly.

00:32:02.770 --> 00:32:06.780
It doesn't make one single
mistake in the entire training

00:32:06.780 --> 00:32:11.070
set which doesn't mean that
it's perfect in the real world.

00:32:11.070 --> 00:32:19.020
As you see on the test
dataset, it has a 98% accuracy.

00:32:19.020 --> 00:32:20.640
But, well, it's something.

00:32:20.640 --> 00:32:25.550
We got 100% at least
on the training.

00:32:25.550 --> 00:32:26.820
All right.

00:32:26.820 --> 00:32:31.830
So we still have something
that is a bit bizarre.

00:32:31.830 --> 00:32:33.840
Look at those two curves.

00:32:33.840 --> 00:32:36.010
This is our error function.

00:32:36.010 --> 00:32:39.070
So the blue curve, the
test error function,

00:32:39.070 --> 00:32:40.900
that is what we minimize.

00:32:40.900 --> 00:32:41.880
OK?

00:32:41.880 --> 00:32:45.660
So as expected, it goes down.

00:32:45.660 --> 00:32:49.620
And the error function
computed on our test data

00:32:49.620 --> 00:32:51.840
at the beginning,
well, it follows.

00:32:51.840 --> 00:32:53.560
That's quite nice.

00:32:53.560 --> 00:32:56.310
And then it disconnects.

00:32:56.310 --> 00:32:59.910
So this is not completely
unexpected, you know.

00:32:59.910 --> 00:33:04.330
We are minimizing the
training at our function.

00:33:04.330 --> 00:33:06.540
That's what we are
actively minimizing.

00:33:06.540 --> 00:33:11.250
We are not doing anything
at all on the test side.

00:33:11.250 --> 00:33:14.850
It's just a byproduct of
the way neural networks work

00:33:14.850 --> 00:33:18.660
that the training you do
on your training data,

00:33:18.660 --> 00:33:24.090
actually carries over to your
test data to the real world.

00:33:24.090 --> 00:33:26.550
Well, it carries
over or it doesn't.

00:33:26.550 --> 00:33:28.890
So as you see here,
until some point,

00:33:28.890 --> 00:33:31.330
it does and then,
there is a disconnect,

00:33:31.330 --> 00:33:34.470
it doesn't carry over anymore.

00:33:34.470 --> 00:33:37.830
You keep optimizing the
error on the training data,

00:33:37.830 --> 00:33:40.170
but it has no positive
effect on the test

00:33:40.170 --> 00:33:43.840
performance, the real
work performance, anymore.

00:33:43.840 --> 00:33:47.460
So if you see curves like
this, you take the textbook,

00:33:47.460 --> 00:33:50.070
you look it up, it's
called overfitting.

00:33:50.070 --> 00:33:52.740
You look at the solutions,
they tell you overfitting,

00:33:52.740 --> 00:33:53.940
you need regularization.

00:33:53.940 --> 00:33:54.440
OK.

00:33:54.440 --> 00:33:55.680
Let's regularize.

00:33:55.680 --> 00:33:58.020
What regularization
options do we have?

00:33:58.020 --> 00:34:01.440
My preferred one
is called dropout.

00:34:01.440 --> 00:34:03.570
It's quite dramatic.

00:34:03.570 --> 00:34:06.540
You shoot the neurons.

00:34:06.540 --> 00:34:08.250
No, really.

00:34:08.250 --> 00:34:10.110
So this is how it works.

00:34:10.110 --> 00:34:13.409
You take your neural network,
and pick a probability,

00:34:13.409 --> 00:34:15.210
let's say 50%.

00:34:15.210 --> 00:34:19.560
So at each training
iteration, you

00:34:19.560 --> 00:34:22.179
will shoot, physically
remove from the network,

00:34:22.179 --> 00:34:24.420
50% percent of your neurons.

00:34:24.420 --> 00:34:28.290
Do the pass, then put them back,
next iteration, again, randomly

00:34:28.290 --> 00:34:30.480
shoot 50% of your neurons.

00:34:30.480 --> 00:34:36.000
Of course, when you test , you
don't test with a half brain

00:34:36.000 --> 00:34:39.540
dead neural network, you
put all the neurons back.

00:34:39.540 --> 00:34:41.580
But that's what you
do for training.

00:34:41.580 --> 00:34:44.520
So in TensorFlow, there
is a very simple function

00:34:44.520 --> 00:34:48.840
to do that, which is called
dropout, That you apply

00:34:48.840 --> 00:34:50.880
at the outputs of the layer.

00:34:50.880 --> 00:34:53.659
And what it simply does is
it takes the probability

00:34:53.659 --> 00:34:56.100
and in the output
of that layer, it

00:34:56.100 --> 00:35:00.300
will replace randomly
some values by zeros

00:35:00.300 --> 00:35:02.940
and small technicality,
it will actually

00:35:02.940 --> 00:35:05.910
boost the remaining
values proportionally

00:35:05.910 --> 00:35:08.100
so that the average
stays constant,

00:35:08.100 --> 00:35:11.160
that's a technicality.

00:35:11.160 --> 00:35:16.810
So why does shooting
neurons help?

00:35:16.810 --> 00:35:20.370
Well, first of all,
let's see if it helps.

00:35:20.370 --> 00:35:24.960
So let's try to recap all
the tricks we tried to play

00:35:24.960 --> 00:35:26.980
with our neural network.

00:35:26.980 --> 00:35:31.530
This is what we had initially
with our five layers

00:35:31.530 --> 00:35:35.820
using the sigmoid as
an activation function.

00:35:35.820 --> 00:35:41.040
The accuracy got up to
97.9% using five layers.

00:35:41.040 --> 00:35:44.100
So first, we replaced the
sigmoid by the relu activation

00:35:44.100 --> 00:35:45.120
function.

00:35:45.120 --> 00:35:49.320
You see, it's faster to
converge at the beginning

00:35:49.320 --> 00:35:52.710
and we actually gained
a couple of fractions

00:35:52.710 --> 00:35:55.610
of percentage of accuracy.

00:35:55.610 --> 00:35:57.330
But we have these messy curves.

00:35:57.330 --> 00:36:03.990
So we train slower using the
exponential learning rate decay

00:36:03.990 --> 00:36:08.490
and we get rid of the noise, and
now we are stable or above 98%

00:36:08.490 --> 00:36:09.690
accuracy.

00:36:09.690 --> 00:36:12.030
But we have that
weird disconnect

00:36:12.030 --> 00:36:15.840
between the error
on our test data

00:36:15.840 --> 00:36:18.960
and the error on
our training data.

00:36:18.960 --> 00:36:22.860
So let us try to add dropout.

00:36:22.860 --> 00:36:25.470
This is what you
get with dropout.

00:36:25.470 --> 00:36:28.770
And actually, the
cross entropy function,

00:36:28.770 --> 00:36:30.930
the test cross entropy
function, the red one

00:36:30.930 --> 00:36:34.470
over there on the right,
has been largely brought

00:36:34.470 --> 00:36:36.060
under control.

00:36:36.060 --> 00:36:38.130
You see, there is
still some disconnect,

00:36:38.130 --> 00:36:40.800
but it's not shooting
up as it was before.

00:36:40.800 --> 00:36:42.520
That's very positive.

00:36:42.520 --> 00:36:44.033
Let's look at the accuracy.

00:36:48.290 --> 00:36:50.950
No improvement.

00:36:50.950 --> 00:36:55.010
Actually, I'm even amazed
that it hasn't gone down

00:36:55.010 --> 00:36:58.560
seeing how brutal this
technique is, you shoot neurons

00:36:58.560 --> 00:37:00.430
while you train.

00:37:00.430 --> 00:37:05.085
But here, I was very
hopeful to get it up.

00:37:05.085 --> 00:37:08.040
No, nothing.

00:37:08.040 --> 00:37:10.740
We have to keep digging.

00:37:10.740 --> 00:37:17.490
So what is really overfitting?

00:37:17.490 --> 00:37:21.640
Let's go beyond the simple
recipe in the textbook.

00:37:21.640 --> 00:37:23.960
Overfitting, in
a neural network,

00:37:23.960 --> 00:37:29.040
is primarily when you give it
too many degrees of freedom.

00:37:29.040 --> 00:37:31.630
Imagine you have so
many neurons and so many

00:37:31.630 --> 00:37:34.530
weights in a neural
network that it's somehow

00:37:34.530 --> 00:37:38.100
feasible to simply store
all the training images

00:37:38.100 --> 00:37:39.730
in those weights and variables.

00:37:39.730 --> 00:37:42.050
You have enough room for that.

00:37:42.050 --> 00:37:45.810
And the neural network could
figure out some cheap trick

00:37:45.810 --> 00:37:49.650
to pattern match the training
images in what it has stored

00:37:49.650 --> 00:37:52.950
and just perfectly recognize
your training images

00:37:52.950 --> 00:37:55.810
because it has stored
copies of all of them.

00:37:55.810 --> 00:37:59.580
Well, if it has enough
space to do that,

00:37:59.580 --> 00:38:03.720
that would not translate to any
kind of recognition performance

00:38:03.720 --> 00:38:05.860
in the real world.

00:38:05.860 --> 00:38:09.210
And that's the trick
about neural networks.

00:38:09.210 --> 00:38:12.840
You have to constrain
their degrees of freedom

00:38:12.840 --> 00:38:16.140
to force them to generalize.

00:38:16.140 --> 00:38:18.300
And mostly, when
you get overfitting

00:38:18.300 --> 00:38:20.040
is because you have
too many neurons.

00:38:20.040 --> 00:38:23.760
You need to get that number
down to force the network

00:38:23.760 --> 00:38:26.550
to produce generalizations
that will then

00:38:26.550 --> 00:38:30.810
produce good predictions,
even in the real world.

00:38:30.810 --> 00:38:34.770
So either you get the
number of neurons down

00:38:34.770 --> 00:38:37.890
or you apply some
trick, like dropout,

00:38:37.890 --> 00:38:42.210
that is supposed to mitigate
the consequences of too

00:38:42.210 --> 00:38:45.960
many degrees of freedom.

00:38:45.960 --> 00:38:50.130
The opposite of too many
neurons if you have a very small

00:38:50.130 --> 00:38:55.320
dataset, well, even if you have
only a small number of neurons,

00:38:55.320 --> 00:38:58.140
if the dataset, the training
dataset is very small,

00:38:58.140 --> 00:39:00.180
it can still fit it all in.

00:39:00.180 --> 00:39:03.810
So that's a general
truth in neural networks.

00:39:03.810 --> 00:39:07.840
You need big datasets
for training.

00:39:07.840 --> 00:39:10.440
And then what happened here?

00:39:10.440 --> 00:39:14.280
We have a big data set,
60,000 digits, that's enough.

00:39:14.280 --> 00:39:17.940
We know that we don't have too
many neurons because we added

00:39:17.940 --> 00:39:21.060
five layers, that's a bit
overkill, but I tried,

00:39:21.060 --> 00:39:24.240
I promise, with four
and three and two.

00:39:24.240 --> 00:39:26.760
And we tried dropout
which is supposed

00:39:26.760 --> 00:39:30.010
to mitigate the fact that
you have too many neurons.

00:39:30.010 --> 00:39:33.420
And it didn't do
anything to the accuracy.

00:39:33.420 --> 00:39:37.050
So the conclusion
here that we come to

00:39:37.050 --> 00:39:43.290
is that our network, the way
it is built, is inadequate.

00:39:43.290 --> 00:39:46.650
It's not capable
by its architecture

00:39:46.650 --> 00:39:50.890
to extract the necessary
information from our data.

00:39:50.890 --> 00:39:56.580
And maybe someone here can
pinpoint something really

00:39:56.580 --> 00:39:59.417
stupid we did at the beginning.

00:39:59.417 --> 00:40:00.250
Someone has an idea?

00:40:02.920 --> 00:40:05.560
Remember, we have images?

00:40:05.560 --> 00:40:10.910
Images with shapes
like curves and lines.

00:40:10.910 --> 00:40:14.750
And we flattened all the
pixels in one big vector.

00:40:14.750 --> 00:40:17.580
So all that shape
information is lost.

00:40:17.580 --> 00:40:18.710
This is terrible.

00:40:18.710 --> 00:40:20.960
That's why we are
performing so badly.

00:40:20.960 --> 00:40:24.770
We lost all of the
shape information.

00:40:24.770 --> 00:40:26.360
So what is the solution?

00:40:26.360 --> 00:40:29.540
Well, people have
invented a different type

00:40:29.540 --> 00:40:32.090
of neural networks to
handle specifically

00:40:32.090 --> 00:40:35.850
images and problems
where shape is important.

00:40:35.850 --> 00:40:39.290
It's called
convolutional networks.

00:40:39.290 --> 00:40:42.540
Here we go back to the
general case of an image,

00:40:42.540 --> 00:40:44.430
of a color image.

00:40:44.430 --> 00:40:50.000
So that's why it has red,
green, and blue components.

00:40:50.000 --> 00:40:54.080
And in a convolutional
network, one neuron

00:40:54.080 --> 00:40:57.710
will still be doing
weighted sums of pixels,

00:40:57.710 --> 00:41:02.210
but only a small patch of
pixels above its head, only

00:41:02.210 --> 00:41:04.690
a small patch.

00:41:04.690 --> 00:41:08.300
And the next neuron
would, again,

00:41:08.300 --> 00:41:11.900
be doing weighted sum of
the small patch of pixels

00:41:11.900 --> 00:41:19.351
above itself, but
using the same weights.

00:41:19.351 --> 00:41:19.850
OK?

00:41:19.850 --> 00:41:21.710
That's the
fundamental difference

00:41:21.710 --> 00:41:23.300
from what we have seen before.

00:41:23.300 --> 00:41:25.340
The second neuron is
using the same weights

00:41:25.340 --> 00:41:26.780
as the first neuron.

00:41:26.780 --> 00:41:29.560
So we are actually taking
just one set of weights

00:41:29.560 --> 00:41:33.260
and we are scanning the
image in both directions,

00:41:33.260 --> 00:41:37.400
using that set of weights
and producing weighted sums.

00:41:37.400 --> 00:41:41.420
So we scan it in both
directions and we obtain

00:41:41.420 --> 00:41:43.550
one layer of weighted sums.

00:41:43.550 --> 00:41:45.630
So how many weights do we have?

00:41:45.630 --> 00:41:47.860
Well, as many weights
as we have input values

00:41:47.860 --> 00:41:51.500
in that little highlighted
cube, that's 4 times 4 times

00:41:51.500 --> 00:41:55.300
3, which is around 48.

00:41:55.300 --> 00:41:56.180
What?

00:41:56.180 --> 00:41:57.470
48?

00:41:57.470 --> 00:42:02.420
We had 8,000 degrees of
freedom in our simplest network

00:42:02.420 --> 00:42:04.700
with just 10 neurons.

00:42:04.700 --> 00:42:07.940
How can it work with
such a drastic reduction

00:42:07.940 --> 00:42:09.860
in the number of weights?

00:42:09.860 --> 00:42:11.510
Well, it won't work.

00:42:11.510 --> 00:42:14.300
We need more degrees of freedom.

00:42:14.300 --> 00:42:16.020
How do we do that?

00:42:16.020 --> 00:42:20.090
Well, we pick a second set
of weights and do this again.

00:42:20.090 --> 00:42:22.280
And we obtain the second--

00:42:22.280 --> 00:42:28.000
let's call it a channel of
values using different weights.

00:42:28.000 --> 00:42:31.010
Now since those are
multi-dimensional matrices,

00:42:31.010 --> 00:42:35.030
it's fairly easy to write
those two matrices as one

00:42:35.030 --> 00:42:37.820
by simply adding a
dimension of dimension two

00:42:37.820 --> 00:42:40.100
because we have
two sets of values.

00:42:40.100 --> 00:42:45.590
And this here will be the
shape of the weight made matrix

00:42:45.590 --> 00:42:49.960
for one convolutional
layer in a neural network.

00:42:54.220 --> 00:42:57.610
Now, we still have
one problem left

00:42:57.610 --> 00:43:02.530
which is that we need to bring
the amount of information down.

00:43:02.530 --> 00:43:04.990
At the end, we still
want only 10 outputs

00:43:04.990 --> 00:43:08.930
with our 10 probabilities to
recognize what this number is.

00:43:08.930 --> 00:43:13.660
So traditionally, this
was achieved by what

00:43:13.660 --> 00:43:17.230
we call a subsampling layer.

00:43:17.230 --> 00:43:19.900
I think it's quite
useful to understand

00:43:19.900 --> 00:43:22.990
how this works because it gives
you a good feeling for what

00:43:22.990 --> 00:43:24.580
this network is doing.

00:43:24.580 --> 00:43:27.100
So basically, we were
scanning the image using

00:43:27.100 --> 00:43:32.050
a set of weights and during
training, these weights

00:43:32.050 --> 00:43:35.239
will actually specialize in
some kind of shape recognizer.

00:43:35.239 --> 00:43:37.030
There will be some
weights that will become

00:43:37.030 --> 00:43:38.770
very sensitive to
horizontal lines

00:43:38.770 --> 00:43:40.210
and some weights
that will become

00:43:40.210 --> 00:43:43.210
very sensitive to
vertical lines, and so on.

00:43:43.210 --> 00:43:47.000
So basically, when you scan
the image, if you simplify,

00:43:47.000 --> 00:43:50.299
you get an output which is
mostly I've seen nothing,

00:43:50.299 --> 00:43:51.840
I've seen nothing,
I've seen nothing,

00:43:51.840 --> 00:43:53.631
oh, I've seen something,
I've seen nothing,

00:43:53.631 --> 00:43:55.700
I've seen nothing, oh,
I've seen something.

00:43:55.700 --> 00:43:59.770
The subsampling basically takes
four of those outputs, two

00:43:59.770 --> 00:44:03.430
by two, and it takes
the maximum value.

00:44:03.430 --> 00:44:07.090
So it retains the biggest
signal of I've seen something

00:44:07.090 --> 00:44:10.610
and passes that down
to the layer below.

00:44:13.150 --> 00:44:15.760
But actually, there's
a much simpler way

00:44:15.760 --> 00:44:19.930
of condensing information.

00:44:19.930 --> 00:44:27.010
What if we simply play with
the stride of the convolution?

00:44:27.010 --> 00:44:31.410
Instead of scanning the
image pixel by pixel,

00:44:31.410 --> 00:44:34.720
we scan it every two pixels,
we jumped by two pixels

00:44:34.720 --> 00:44:36.460
between each weighted sum.

00:44:36.460 --> 00:44:39.820
Well, mechanically,
instead of obtaining 28

00:44:39.820 --> 00:44:44.110
by 28 output values, we obtain
only 14 by 14 output values.

00:44:44.110 --> 00:44:46.720
So we have condensed
our information.

00:44:46.720 --> 00:44:51.010
And mostly today, I'm not
saying this is better,

00:44:51.010 --> 00:44:52.950
but it's just simpler.

00:44:52.950 --> 00:44:56.650
And mostly today, people who
build convolutional networks

00:44:56.650 --> 00:45:00.370
just use convolutional
layers and play

00:45:00.370 --> 00:45:06.900
with the step to condense the
information and it's simpler.

00:45:06.900 --> 00:45:11.600
You don't need, in this way, to
have these subsampling layers.

00:45:11.600 --> 00:45:15.170
So this is the network that I
would like to build with you.

00:45:15.170 --> 00:45:16.405
Let's go through it.

00:45:16.405 --> 00:45:21.850
There is a first convolutional
layer that uses patches of five

00:45:21.850 --> 00:45:22.510
by five.

00:45:22.510 --> 00:45:25.840
I'm reading through
the W1 tensor.

00:45:25.840 --> 00:45:28.030
And we have seen
that in this shape,

00:45:28.030 --> 00:45:32.410
the two first digits is the
size of the patch you pass.

00:45:32.410 --> 00:45:36.340
The third digits is
the number of channels

00:45:36.340 --> 00:45:37.780
it's reading from the input.

00:45:37.780 --> 00:45:40.340
So here I'm back
to my real example.

00:45:40.340 --> 00:45:41.830
This is a grayscale image.

00:45:41.830 --> 00:45:44.160
It has one value per pixel.

00:45:44.160 --> 00:45:47.720
So I'm reading one
channel of information.

00:45:47.720 --> 00:45:52.600
And I will be applying four
of those patches to my image.

00:45:52.600 --> 00:45:56.390
So I obtain four channels
of output values.

00:45:56.390 --> 00:45:57.610
OK?

00:45:57.610 --> 00:46:03.970
Now second convolutional later,
this time, my stride is two.

00:46:03.970 --> 00:46:09.650
So here, my outputs become
plains of 14 by 14 values.

00:46:09.650 --> 00:46:10.930
So let's go through it.

00:46:10.930 --> 00:46:13.310
I'm applying patches
of four by four.

00:46:13.310 --> 00:46:16.210
I'm reading in four
channels of values

00:46:16.210 --> 00:46:18.580
because that's why I
output in the first layer.

00:46:18.580 --> 00:46:22.840
And this time, I will be
using eight different batches,

00:46:22.840 --> 00:46:26.290
so I will actually produce
eight different channels

00:46:26.290 --> 00:46:28.350
of weighted sums.

00:46:28.350 --> 00:46:30.190
Nextly, again, a stride of two.

00:46:30.190 --> 00:46:35.170
That's why I'm getting down
from 14 by 14 to seven by seven.

00:46:35.170 --> 00:46:37.510
Batch is of four
by four, reading

00:46:37.510 --> 00:46:39.850
in eight channels of
values because that's

00:46:39.850 --> 00:46:41.260
what I had in the
previous layer,

00:46:41.260 --> 00:46:45.640
and outputting 12
channels of values

00:46:45.640 --> 00:46:49.340
this time because I used
12 different batches.

00:46:49.340 --> 00:46:53.590
And now I apply a
fully connected layer.

00:46:53.590 --> 00:46:56.080
So the kind of layer
we've seen before.

00:46:56.080 --> 00:46:57.040
OK?

00:46:57.040 --> 00:47:01.510
This fully connected layer,
I remember the differences

00:47:01.510 --> 00:47:04.840
in this one, each neuron
does a weighted sum

00:47:04.840 --> 00:47:10.300
of all the values in the
little cube of values above,

00:47:10.300 --> 00:47:12.610
not just a batch,
all the values.

00:47:12.610 --> 00:47:16.380
In the next neuron in the
fully connected network does,

00:47:16.380 --> 00:47:19.990
again, a weighted sum of all the
values using its own weights.

00:47:19.990 --> 00:47:21.760
It's not sharing weights.

00:47:21.760 --> 00:47:27.790
That's the normal neural network
layer as we have seen before.

00:47:27.790 --> 00:47:33.260
And finally, I apply my softmax
layer with my 10 outputs.

00:47:33.260 --> 00:47:34.510
All right.

00:47:34.510 --> 00:47:38.140
So can we write
this in TensorFlow?

00:47:38.140 --> 00:47:43.450
Well, we need one set of weights
and biases for each layer.

00:47:43.450 --> 00:47:49.220
The only difference is that
for the convolutional layers,

00:47:49.220 --> 00:47:52.950
our weights will have
this specific shape

00:47:52.950 --> 00:47:54.190
that we have seen before.

00:47:54.190 --> 00:47:57.570
So choose numbers
for the filter size,

00:47:57.570 --> 00:47:59.700
one number for the
number of input channels,

00:47:59.700 --> 00:48:01.637
and one number for
the number of batches

00:48:01.637 --> 00:48:03.720
which corresponds to the
number of output channels

00:48:03.720 --> 00:48:05.940
that you produce.

00:48:05.940 --> 00:48:09.900
For our normal layers, we
have the weights and bias as

00:48:09.900 --> 00:48:11.730
defined as before.

00:48:11.730 --> 00:48:16.680
And so you see this truncated
normal thingy up there?

00:48:16.680 --> 00:48:17.980
That's just random.

00:48:17.980 --> 00:48:18.690
OK?

00:48:18.690 --> 00:48:21.150
Its a complicated
way of saying random.

00:48:21.150 --> 00:48:26.150
So we initialize those weights
to random values, initially.

00:48:26.150 --> 00:48:30.340
And now this is what our
model will look like.

00:48:30.340 --> 00:48:34.680
So TensorFlow has these
helpful conv2d function.

00:48:34.680 --> 00:48:38.700
If you give it the weights'
matrix and a batch of images,

00:48:38.700 --> 00:48:40.770
it will scan them
in both directions.

00:48:40.770 --> 00:48:43.800
Its just a double loop to scan
the image in both directions

00:48:43.800 --> 00:48:46.530
and produce the weighted sums.

00:48:46.530 --> 00:48:48.090
So we do those weighted sums.

00:48:48.090 --> 00:48:49.080
We had a bias .

00:48:49.080 --> 00:48:52.530
We feed this through
an activation function,

00:48:52.530 --> 00:48:56.110
in this case, the relu,
and that's our outputs.

00:48:56.110 --> 00:48:58.755
And again, the way of
stacking these layers

00:48:58.755 --> 00:49:01.980
is to feed why one,
the first output,

00:49:01.980 --> 00:49:03.990
has the input of the next layer.

00:49:07.020 --> 00:49:08.310
All right.

00:49:08.310 --> 00:49:12.570
After our three
convolutional layers,

00:49:12.570 --> 00:49:18.300
we need to do a
weighted sum this time

00:49:18.300 --> 00:49:23.220
of all the values in this seven
by seven by 12 little cube.

00:49:23.220 --> 00:49:25.970
So to achieve that, we
will flatten this cube

00:49:25.970 --> 00:49:29.040
as one big vector of values.

00:49:29.040 --> 00:49:31.740
That's what the
Reshape here does.

00:49:31.740 --> 00:49:35.690
And then, two additional lines
that you should recognize,

00:49:35.690 --> 00:49:41.430
those are normal neural network
layers as we have seen before.

00:49:41.430 --> 00:49:43.140
All right.

00:49:43.140 --> 00:49:45.400
How does this work?

00:49:45.400 --> 00:49:48.720
So this time, it takes
a little bit more time

00:49:48.720 --> 00:49:53.490
to process so I have a video.

00:49:53.490 --> 00:49:55.870
You see the accuracy's
shooting up really fast.

00:49:55.870 --> 00:49:57.030
I will have to zoom.

00:49:57.030 --> 00:50:01.770
And the promise to 99% accuracy
is actually not too far.

00:50:01.770 --> 00:50:03.100
We're getting there.

00:50:03.100 --> 00:50:05.185
We're getting there.

00:50:05.185 --> 00:50:06.060
Are we getting there?

00:50:08.960 --> 00:50:11.400
We're not getting there.

00:50:11.400 --> 00:50:12.530
Oh, damn.

00:50:12.530 --> 00:50:14.910
I'm so disappointed again.

00:50:14.910 --> 00:50:19.520
I really wanted to bring
this to 99% accuracy.

00:50:19.520 --> 00:50:23.540
We'll have to do
something more, 98.9.

00:50:23.540 --> 00:50:26.360
Dammit, that was so close.

00:50:26.360 --> 00:50:26.940
All right.

00:50:30.470 --> 00:50:31.580
Yes.

00:50:31.580 --> 00:50:32.900
Exactly.

00:50:32.900 --> 00:50:35.600
This should be your WTF moment.

00:50:35.600 --> 00:50:36.740
What is that?

00:50:36.740 --> 00:50:39.960
On the cross entropy loss curve.

00:50:39.960 --> 00:50:41.370
OK, let me zoom on it.

00:50:44.520 --> 00:50:45.270
You see that?

00:50:45.270 --> 00:50:47.370
That disconnect?

00:50:47.370 --> 00:50:48.780
Do we have a solution for this?

00:50:51.730 --> 00:50:52.830
Dropout.

00:50:52.830 --> 00:50:53.490
Yes.

00:50:53.490 --> 00:50:54.980
Let's go shooting our neurons.

00:50:54.980 --> 00:50:58.720
It didn't work last time,
maybe this time it will.

00:50:58.720 --> 00:51:03.980
So actually, what we will do
here, it's a little trick.

00:51:03.980 --> 00:51:06.630
It's almost a
methodology for coming up

00:51:06.630 --> 00:51:10.350
with the ideal neural network
for a given situation.

00:51:10.350 --> 00:51:14.220
And what I like doing is to
restrict the degrees of freedom

00:51:14.220 --> 00:51:18.390
until it's apparent
that it's not optimal.

00:51:18.390 --> 00:51:19.740
It's hurting the performance.

00:51:19.740 --> 00:51:23.670
Here, I know that I
can get about 99%.

00:51:23.670 --> 00:51:26.490
So I erased a
little bit too much.

00:51:26.490 --> 00:51:30.610
And from that point, I give
it a little bit more freedom

00:51:30.610 --> 00:51:35.220
and apply dropout to make sure
that this additional freedom

00:51:35.220 --> 00:51:38.100
will not result in overfitting.

00:51:38.100 --> 00:51:40.320
And that's basically
how you obtain

00:51:40.320 --> 00:51:43.780
a pretty optimal neural
network for a given problem.

00:51:43.780 --> 00:51:45.600
So that's what I have done here.

00:51:45.600 --> 00:51:48.990
You see, the batches are a
slightly bigger, six, six,

00:51:48.990 --> 00:51:50.820
five, five, four,
four, instead of five,

00:51:50.820 --> 00:51:53.240
five, four, four, and so on.

00:51:53.240 --> 00:51:57.150
And I've used a
lot more batches.

00:51:57.150 --> 00:52:03.240
So six patches in the first
layer, 12 in the second layer,

00:52:03.240 --> 00:52:09.370
and 24 in the third layer,
instead of four, eight, and 12.

00:52:09.370 --> 00:52:12.930
And, I applied dropout in
the fully connected layer.

00:52:12.930 --> 00:52:16.650
So why not in the other layers?

00:52:16.650 --> 00:52:19.290
I tried both, it's
possible to apply dropout

00:52:19.290 --> 00:52:21.120
in convolutional layers.

00:52:21.120 --> 00:52:24.720
But actually, if you count
the number of neurons,

00:52:24.720 --> 00:52:29.160
there is a lot more neurons
in the fully connected layer.

00:52:29.160 --> 00:52:32.610
So it's a lot more efficient
to be shooting them there.

00:52:32.610 --> 00:52:34.950
I mean, it hurts a
little bit too much

00:52:34.950 --> 00:52:38.110
to shoot neurons where you
have only a few of them.

00:52:38.110 --> 00:52:44.040
So with this, let's
run this again.

00:52:44.040 --> 00:52:46.770
So again, the accuracy
shoots up very fast.

00:52:46.770 --> 00:52:48.090
I will have to zoom in.

00:52:48.090 --> 00:52:51.510
Look where the 99%
is and we are above!

00:52:51.510 --> 00:52:53.220
Yes!

00:52:53.220 --> 00:52:56.190
[APPLAUSE]

00:52:56.190 --> 00:52:58.800
Thank you.

00:52:58.800 --> 00:53:02.820
I promised you will get
above 99 and we are actually

00:53:02.820 --> 00:53:04.860
quite comfortably above.

00:53:04.860 --> 00:53:08.160
We get to 99.3%.

00:53:08.160 --> 00:53:13.920
In this time, let's see what
our dropout actually did.

00:53:13.920 --> 00:53:17.790
So this is what we had
with a five layer network

00:53:17.790 --> 00:53:20.650
and already a little
more degrees of freedom.

00:53:20.650 --> 00:53:25.080
So more patches in each layer.

00:53:25.080 --> 00:53:28.290
You see, we are
already above 99%.

00:53:28.290 --> 00:53:35.550
But we have this big
disconnect between the test

00:53:35.550 --> 00:53:37.820
and the training cross entropy.

00:53:37.820 --> 00:53:41.460
Letters apply dropout, boom.

00:53:41.460 --> 00:53:45.240
The test cross entropy function
is brought in under control.

00:53:45.240 --> 00:53:47.040
It's not shooting up as much.

00:53:47.040 --> 00:53:49.620
And look, this time,
we actually had

00:53:49.620 --> 00:53:51.720
a problem and this fixed it.

00:53:51.720 --> 00:53:58.290
With just applying dropout,
we got 2/10 of a percent

00:53:58.290 --> 00:53:59.620
more accuracy.

00:53:59.620 --> 00:54:04.380
And here, we are fighting
for the last percent,

00:54:04.380 --> 00:54:05.910
between 99 and 100.

00:54:05.910 --> 00:54:12.330
So getting 2/10 is enormous
with just a little trick.

00:54:12.330 --> 00:54:13.150
All right.

00:54:13.150 --> 00:54:14.860
So there we have it.

00:54:14.860 --> 00:54:17.730
We built this network and
brought it all the way

00:54:17.730 --> 00:54:21.090
to 99% accuracy.

00:54:21.090 --> 00:54:26.460
The Cliff's Notes
is just a summary.

00:54:26.460 --> 00:54:33.510
And to finish, so this was
mostly about TensorFlow.

00:54:33.510 --> 00:54:36.600
We also have a couple of
pre-trained APIs, which

00:54:36.600 --> 00:54:40.530
you can use just as APIs if
your problem is standard enough

00:54:40.530 --> 00:54:43.860
to fit into one of those Cloud
Vision, Cloud Speech, Natural

00:54:43.860 --> 00:54:46.810
Language, or Translate APIs.

00:54:46.810 --> 00:54:51.960
And if you want to run your
TensorFlow jobs in the cloud,

00:54:51.960 --> 00:54:57.570
we also have this
Cloud ML Engine service

00:54:57.570 --> 00:54:59.820
that allows you to
execute your TensorFlow

00:54:59.820 --> 00:55:02.010
jobs in the cloud for training.

00:55:02.010 --> 00:55:04.920
And what is even more
important, with just the click

00:55:04.920 --> 00:55:07.890
of a button, you can
take a train model

00:55:07.890 --> 00:55:11.040
and push it to
production behind an API

00:55:11.040 --> 00:55:15.420
and start serving predictions
from the model in the cloud.

00:55:15.420 --> 00:55:18.135
So I think that's a
little technical detail,

00:55:18.135 --> 00:55:19.830
but from an engineering
perspective,

00:55:19.830 --> 00:55:23.040
it's quite significant that you
have a very easy way of pushing

00:55:23.040 --> 00:55:25.200
something to prod.

00:55:25.200 --> 00:55:25.980
Thank you.

00:55:25.980 --> 00:55:29.580
You have the code on GitHub
and this slide deck is freely

00:55:29.580 --> 00:55:31.740
available at that URL.

00:55:31.740 --> 00:55:33.990
And with that, we have
five minutes for questions,

00:55:33.990 --> 00:55:35.280
if you have any.

00:55:35.280 --> 00:55:36.580
[APPLAUSE]

00:55:36.580 --> 00:55:37.080
Thank you.

00:55:41.580 --> 00:55:44.330
